*This project has been created as part of the 42 curriculum by maliuten.*

# Get Next Line

## Description

Get Next Line is a C project whose goal is to implement a function that reads and returns one line at a time from a file descriptor.

The function prototype is:

char *get_next_line(int fd);

Each call to get_next_line() returns the next available line, including the newline character (`\n`) when one is present.

If the end of the file is reached and the last line does not end with a newline, the remaining characters are returned as the final line.

When there is nothing left to read, or when an error occurs, the function returns `NULL`.

The project also introduces the use of static variables in C in order to preserve unread data between consecutive calls to get_next_line().

## Files

- `get_next_line.c` — main get_next_line() function
- `get_next_line_utils.c` — helper functions used by get_next_line()
- `get_next_line.h` — function prototypes, required includes and BUFFER_SIZE definition
- `README.md` — project documentation

## Instructions

Compile the project using:

cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror -D BUFFER_SIZE=42 get_next_line.c get_next_line_utils.c main.c

`BUFFER_SIZE` defines how many bytes `read()` may attempt to read at one time.

It can be changed during compilation, for example:

cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror -D BUFFER_SIZE=1 get_next_line.c get_next_line_utils.c main.c

or:

cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror -D BUFFER_SIZE=100 get_next_line.c get_next_line_utils.c main.c

The project can also be compiled without defining BUFFER_SIZE explicitly. In that case, the default value defined in `get_next_line.h` is used.

Run the compiled program with:

./a.out

A test `main.c` can be used locally to open a file and repeatedly call get_next_line(). The test file and main function are not part of the mandatory submitted files.

## Algorithm

The implementation uses a static variable called `stash`.

The stash stores data that has already been read from the file descriptor but has not yet been returned to the caller.

The algorithm works as follows:

1. Check whether the stash already contains a newline character.
2. If no newline is present, allocate a temporary buffer and call `read()`.
3. Append the newly read buffer to the existing stash.
4. Continue reading until:
   - a newline is found,
   - the end of the file is reached,
   - or an error occurs.
5. Once enough data has been collected, extract one line from the beginning of the stash.
6. The returned line includes the newline character when one is present.
7. Everything located after the returned line is saved as the new stash.
8. The new stash remains available because it is declared as a static variable.
9. On the next call to get_next_line(), the function continues using the remaining data stored in the stash.
10. When there is no remaining data and `read()` reaches the end of the file, the function returns `NULL`.

For example, if the stash contains:

apple\nban

the function returns:

apple\n

and keeps:

ban

in the stash for the next call.

This approach avoids reading the entire file at once and only reads additional data when necessary.

## Memory Management

Memory is dynamically allocated for:

- the temporary read buffer,
- the accumulated stash,
- the line returned by get_next_line(),
- and the remaining stash after a line is extracted.

Old dynamically allocated stash memory is freed whenever a new version replaces it.

The caller is responsible for freeing every non-NULL line returned by get_next_line().

Memory is also cleaned up when a read error occurs or when stored data is no longer needed.

## Allowed External Functions

The implementation only uses the external functions permitted by the subject:

- `read`
- `malloc`
- `free`

Libft is not used.

## Resources

Resources used while studying and implementing the project:

- Get Next Line subject, version 14.2
- `man 2 read`
- `man 3 malloc`
- `man 3 free`
- C documentation concerning pointers, strings, static variables and dynamic memory allocation

## AI Usage

AI was used as a learning and debugging aid during the project.

It was used to:

- understand file descriptors and the behavior of `read()`;
- understand static variables and why a stash is required;
- understand BUFFER_SIZE and preprocessor macros;
- review memory ownership and dynamic allocation;
- explain some of the compiler errors and help identify bugs during testing.

The implementation was developed on my own, step by step while reviewing the behavior and purpose of each part of the algorithm.